WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.980
How do you watch one motor work when many act together?

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:08.440
Inside a cell, crowded filaments and motors blur into one average.

3
00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.020
Pull purified parts into a glass microscope chamber instead.

4
00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:17.993
Working outside the cell, in vitro, lets you choose each ingredient.

5
00:00:19.393 --> 00:00:24.553
Add purified polar tracks, actin or microtubules, each with two distinct ends.

6
00:00:25.793 --> 00:00:30.513
Use fluorescence or dark-field microscopy so single tracks shine in the dark.

7
00:00:31.773 --> 00:00:33.833
Add ATP, and watch motion begin.

8
00:00:34.917 --> 00:00:39.977
On its track, each motor foot grips, swings while gripping, lets go, and re-cocks.

9
00:00:41.337 --> 00:00:44.757
One step's work cannot exceed the energy of one ATP.

10
00:00:46.397 --> 00:00:50.957
Force times step is at most about 80 to 100 pN·nm.

11
00:00:51.957 --> 00:00:56.777
Watching purified parts one by one reveals motion bulk averages hide.

12
00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:02.730
If one foot always grips, one kinesin-1 or myosin V walks alone.

13
00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.510
Muscle myosin II also has two heads.

14
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:12.270
In our chamber, could one myosin II molecule carry a vesicle along actin?

15
00:01:17.783 --> 00:01:21.683
No: myosin II feet spend most of each cycle off the track.

16
00:01:22.863 --> 00:01:27.103
A lone molecule would drift off, so muscle myosin II works in a crowd.

17
00:01:28.563 --> 00:01:31.463
Purified-motor tests also catch direction exceptions.

18
00:01:32.803 --> 00:01:36.423
Purified myosin VI moves toward actin's pointed end.

19
00:01:36.423 --> 00:01:41.983
Fruit-fly kinesin ncd moves toward the microtubule minus end.

20
00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:46.033
Still, glowing spots under a microscope can mislead.

21
00:01:47.553 --> 00:01:52.193
Clusters below the microscope's resolution look round, just like liquid droplets.

22
00:01:53.633 --> 00:01:57.793
Drifting clusters look merged unless they relax into one round body.

23
00:01:59.153 --> 00:02:05.133
Bleaching fluorescence and watching glow return, called FRAP, measures turnover that

24
00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:07.133
binding and unbinding also give.

25
00:02:08.217 --> 00:02:13.497
Bleaching half a large spot tests whether molecules mix inside it faster than they

26
00:02:13.497 --> 00:02:19.877
cross its edge. Faster inside mixing favours a liquid droplet without proving one.

27
00:02:21.397 --> 00:02:23.017
Motor chambers have their own limit.

28
00:02:24.237 --> 00:02:29.397
A chamber result describes motors outside the cell, at low load unless a load is

29
00:02:29.397 --> 00:02:30.397
stated.

30
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.120
With those guards, microscope chambers unlocked three breakthroughs.

31
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.980
Summers and Gibbons briefly treated sea-urchin sperm axonemes, the cores of their

32
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:44.040
tails, with the protein-cutting enzyme trypsin.

33
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.020
It disrupted what resists sliding.

34
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:55.040
With 0.1 mM ATP under dark-field microscopy, groups of microtubule doublets slid

35
00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:60.000
out. So dynein slides microtubules rather than shortening them.

36
00:03:00.983 --> 00:03:06.743
Second, in 1984, Mitchison and Kirschner inferred dynamic instability from

37
00:03:06.743 --> 00:03:08.163
microtubule populations.

38
00:03:09.563 --> 00:03:12.883
Their ends switch between steady growth and rapid shortening.

39
00:03:14.203 --> 00:03:18.363
Microscopy of single purified ends soon showed those switches directly.

40
00:03:19.533 --> 00:03:25.753
Third, Noji and Yoshida fixed ATP synthase heads to glass with a fluorescent actin

41
00:03:25.753 --> 00:03:32.373
filament. The heads spun the filament in discrete 120° -degree steps, one per ATP.

42
00:03:33.453 --> 00:03:37.753
Isolated on glass, single parts show what the crowd's average hides.
